{"author":"John Wesley","book":"Notes on the Bible","chapter":750,"classification":{"bucket":"Christian Classics / Commentaries","canonicalStatus":"noncanonical","contentKind":"commentary","cautionReason":"","doctrinalStatus":"historical","labels":["christian-classics","commentaries"],"severity":0},"id":"classics:wesley:Ps.13","lineage":[],"metadata":{"author":"John Wesley","authorshipDateRange":"18th century","genre":"Commentaries","tradition":"Methodist","translator":null,"edition":"CCEL edition; source omits 2 Samuel 24","rights":"Public Domain","source":{"id":"wesley","provider":"CCEL","sourceUrl":"https://www.ccel.org/ccel/wesley/notes.html","title":"Notes on the Bible"}},"originalBook":"Psalms","sourceVolumeId":"wesley","source":{"id":"wesley","sourceUrl":"https://www.ccel.org/ccel/wesley/notes.html","title":"Notes on the Bible"},"title":"Psalms 13","reference":"Psalms 13","notes":[],"verses":[{"verse":1,"text":"David complains to God, ver.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":2,"text":"1, 2.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":3,"text":"Prays for help, ver.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":4,"text":"3, 4.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":5,"text":"Rejoices in hope, ver.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":6,"text":"5, 6.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":7,"text":"To the chief musician, A psalm of David.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":8,"text":"2.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":2,"scriptureVerseEnd":2},{"verse":9,"text":"How long - Shall I be in such perplexities, not knowing what course to take?","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":2,"scriptureVerseEnd":2},{"verse":10,"text":"3.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":3,"scriptureVerseEnd":3},{"verse":11,"text":"Lighten - Revive and comfort, and deliver me from the darkness of death, which is ready to come upon me.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":3,"scriptureVerseEnd":3},{"verse":12,"text":"6.","sourceParagraph":4,"scriptureVerseStart":6,"scriptureVerseEnd":6},{"verse":13,"text":"I will sing - It is a common thing for David and other prophets to speak of future deliverances as if they were already come, that so they may signify both the infallible certainty of the thing, and their firm assurance thereof.","sourceParagraph":4,"scriptureVerseStart":6,"scriptureVerseEnd":6}],"scriptureReference":{"book":"Psalms","chapter":13}}